83
00:04:46,269 --> 00:04:52,369
so here a manual log correlation
that's, that's what is slowing it down

84
00:04:52,439 --> 00:04:54,920
and making the diagnostics unreliable.

85
00:04:55,559 --> 00:05:01,509
and inconsistent sampling also, it drops
the exact failure you needed to see.

86
00:05:01,890 --> 00:05:05,189
So because sampling decisions were made
per service, not per trace, these gaps,

87
00:05:06,189 --> 00:05:09,229
they quietly lead you
to the wrong root cause.

88
00:05:10,129 --> 00:05:12,669
The fix here isn't,
like a fourth dashboard.

89
00:05:13,029 --> 00:05:18,129
we want a shared identity that flows
across every service, so the three

90
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:23,319
pillars finally point at the same thing
And here we are following, the shared

91
00:05:23,349 --> 00:05:26,839
identity, which is, a W3C trace context.

92
00:05:27,399 --> 00:05:29,419
this, is a web standard.

93
00:05:29,489 --> 00:05:31,639
It's not a vendor feature.

94
00:05:32,099 --> 00:05:38,169
it gives, it gives software systems like
a single universal way to pass tracking

95
00:05:38,169 --> 00:05:39,869
information between the services.

96
00:05:40,419 --> 00:05:46,029
Which means it's vendor-agnostic,
and it propagates a trace ID,

97
00:05:46,339 --> 00:05:50,549
and a span ID across, HTTP
calls, in a vendor-neutral way.

98
00:05:51,599 --> 00:05:54,579
This, header that you are
seeing is a trace parent.

99
00:05:54,969 --> 00:05:56,459
It carries four things.

100
00:05:56,929 --> 00:06:01,889
One is the version, and then the second
is a globally unique trace ID that

101
00:06:01,909 --> 00:06:04,599
stays constant across the whole request.

102
00:06:05,059 --> 00:06:08,689
And then there is a span ID,
like for the current hop.

103
00:06:09,019 --> 00:06:15,379
It's, specific to the service and specific
to that particular request, and flags.

104
00:06:16,109 --> 00:06:20,449
So every service reads it, and they
pass it on to the next service.

105
00:06:21,199 --> 00:06:22,619
If you see, the flow is simple.

106
00:06:22,729 --> 00:06:26,339
the ingress generates the identity,
every service propagates it

107
00:06:26,339 --> 00:06:30,629
unchanged, and the collector
assembles a full trace at the end.

108
00:06:31,009 --> 00:06:36,039
and here now the question is why
build on, a standard instead of

109
00:06:36,039 --> 00:06:37,979
rolling your own correlation ID?

110
00:06:39,409 --> 00:06:45,039
So the reason is that you are not
logged into any one vendor's SDK.

111
00:06:45,609 --> 00:06:51,029
You can run one service instrumented with
the OpenTelemetry SDK, another like behind

112
00:06:51,029 --> 00:06:57,099
a service mesh, or a third with a vendor
agent, and they all speak the same header.

113
00:06:58,029 --> 00:07:05,599
So here, the identity survi-survives
every single hop across every language

114
00:07:06,249 --> 00:07:08,369
and, framework in your stack as well.

115
00:07:08,939 --> 00:07:14,669
So this is the foundation everything
else sits on Here I have mapped,

116
00:07:14,779 --> 00:07:19,389
like three decisions that turn a
standard into something reliable.

117
00:07:20,059 --> 00:07:23,699
The first one is trace identity
that is being generated at the

118
00:07:24,029 --> 00:07:29,099
ingress gateway, and every inbound
request carries a globally unique ID

119
00:07:29,139 --> 00:07:30,799
before it touches a single service.

120
00:07:31,669 --> 00:07:34,869
and no request, it
starts, life untraceable.

121
00:07:35,959 --> 00:07:41,489
The second one, this is the one I'll
defend the hardest, context propagation.

122
00:07:41,799 --> 00:07:42,819
This is mandatory.

123
00:07:43,129 --> 00:07:47,289
Like, if the, if the propagation
is optional, you get diagnostic

124
00:07:47,299 --> 00:07:51,619
gaps, and a gap is exactly
where your worst incidents hide.

125
00:07:52,119 --> 00:07:56,909
So teams that treat it as a
nice to have, discover the hole

126
00:07:56,909 --> 00:07:58,519
at the worst possible moment.

127
00:07:59,029 --> 00:08:02,869
And the third one is sampling,
which is the export targets,

128
00:08:02,919 --> 00:08:06,069
verbosity, all config-driven.

129
00:08:06,069 --> 00:08:07,059
They are not hard-coded.

130
00:08:07,599 --> 00:08:11,079
That means you can tune telemetry
in production without a redeploy.

131
00:08:11,609 --> 00:08:16,389
So during an incident, that's the
difference between minutes and hours

132
00:08:18,287 --> 00:08:20,237
Now, let me show you the whole path.

133
00:08:20,727 --> 00:08:26,467
So a request comes in, ingress injects
a trace parent, and then the same trace

134
00:08:26,467 --> 00:08:30,117
ID propagates through authentication
service, cart service, payment

135
00:08:30,117 --> 00:08:36,797
service unchanged, till the end, and
along the, synchronous request path.

136
00:08:37,297 --> 00:08:42,847
So that's the top row, like you-- like
where you are seeing there are hard lines.

137
00:08:43,447 --> 00:08:48,947
Now, there-- here is a detail that
matters most for this audience, and

138
00:08:49,037 --> 00:08:52,597
it's a difference between a design that
helps you and the one that hurts you.

139
00:08:53,037 --> 00:08:58,087
So each service, it exports
its spans asynchronously of the

140
00:08:58,097 --> 00:09:02,187
request path, and, they send it
to the, open telemetry collector.

141
00:09:03,437 --> 00:09:08,067
So l-like these other arrows,
like, which you see, the dotted

142
00:09:08,067 --> 00:09:11,177
ones, these, are the span exports.

143
00:09:11,177 --> 00:09:15,757
Go-- Th-these are going, these are
going down, the, the other span

144
00:09:15,757 --> 00:09:19,367
exports, and they're completely
decoupled from that request.

145
00:09:19,987 --> 00:09:24,597
And the solid arrows across the top,
they are the actual user request.

146
00:09:24,927 --> 00:09:26,157
They're moving from one service

147
00:09:27,157 --> 00:09:27,227
to another.

148
00:09:27,227 --> 00:09:32,297
And here, a service, it records its
span and hands it to, a local buffer.

149
00:09:32,897 --> 00:09:37,047
Then the request moves on immediately
without waiting for anything to

150
00:09:37,047 --> 00:09:40,907
be written or without waiting
for it to be shipped anywhere.

151
00:09:42,047 --> 00:09:47,007
The collector then assembles the
trace, and it ships it to the back end.

152
00:09:47,017 --> 00:09:50,717
Here, the back end can be any
observability tools where you,

153
00:09:50,797 --> 00:09:52,367
can query by the trace ID.

154
00:09:53,107 --> 00:09:58,497
And because that export is off the
request path, like if the collector

155
00:09:58,557 --> 00:10:02,807
goes down or the back end is slow
or the network to your observability

156
00:10:02,807 --> 00:10:08,767
vendor is having a bad day or, like,
you, you drop spans, but still, like,

157
00:10:08,767 --> 00:10:10,717
your, your request still succeeds.

158
00:10:11,267 --> 00:10:16,527
So your monitoring can be completely on
fire, and your users never notice it.

159
00:10:17,077 --> 00:10:22,887
That dashed line in your zero request
impact, the, the dash-dashed line here,

160
00:10:23,427 --> 00:10:25,937
it's your zero request impact guarantee.

161
00:10:26,407 --> 00:10:30,847
And, we will, we'll come back to it,
when I'm talking about the resilience.

162
00:10:31,257 --> 00:10:34,338
let's just move on to,
the security, bucket.

163
00:10:34,358 --> 00:10:41,440
Like, here Now, like, the rich
trace, metadata, it is useful.

164
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:48,110
But, uns-unrestricted metadata
propagation is how you leak PR two

165
00:10:48,110 --> 00:10:52,640
credentials or internal topology
straight into your telemetry pipeline.

166
00:10:53,140 --> 00:10:56,040
So we invert the default.

167
00:10:56,180 --> 00:10:59,910
Instead of blocking bad fields,
we allow only the vetted ones.

168
00:11:00,520 --> 00:11:01,830
Here, the default is deny.

169
00:11:02,900 --> 00:11:05,680
Think about how metadata usually grows.

170
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:10,950
An engineer is debugging something
that adds a span attribute to help.

171
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:16,740
Maybe the full request body or
maybe a user object, or it can be an

172
00:11:16,950 --> 00:11:21,910
authentication, or, or authentication
header, and they ship it and they move on.

173
00:11:22,970 --> 00:11:24,310
It works, so it stays.

174
00:11:24,890 --> 00:11:31,260
So multiply that across dozens of teams,
like, over the couple of years, and

175
00:11:31,260 --> 00:11:36,040
your trace store quietly becomes a copy
of your most sensitive data sitting in

176
00:11:36,180 --> 00:11:38,680
your system your whole org can query.

177
00:11:39,050 --> 00:11:39,800
That's the leak.

178
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:42,350
Like, nobody, like, decided to do it.

179
00:11:42,890 --> 00:11:44,896
so we invert the default.

180
00:11:44,896 --> 00:11:46,387
Here are the nine, like,
approved, operational fields.

181
00:11:46,387 --> 00:11:46,527
the

182
00:11:47,527 --> 00:11:51,587
trace ID, span ID, parent span
ID, and then the service name,

183
00:11:52,657 --> 00:11:57,787
operation name, timestamps, duration,
status, and HTTP response code.

184
00:11:58,257 --> 00:12:02,347
So every one of them went through
an explicit approval gate.

185
00:12:02,957 --> 00:12:04,397
Notice what's on this list.

186
00:12:04,437 --> 00:12:05,627
It's all, like, structured.

187
00:12:05,887 --> 00:12:11,537
Like, it tells you what happened and
how long it took, and nothing about

188
00:12:11,597 --> 00:12:14,277
who the user was or what they sent.

189
00:12:14,637 --> 00:12:18,557
And everything else, like the
payloads, headers, user data, they

190
00:12:18,557 --> 00:12:20,387
never enter the, pipeline at all.

191
00:12:21,107 --> 00:12:25,357
You get diagnostic richness without
turning your trace store into a compliance

192
00:12:25,407 --> 00:12:29,937
liability and without depending on
every engineer to remember the rules

193
00:12:31,931 --> 00:12:34,391
Next, I'll talk about, the resilience.

194
00:12:35,331 --> 00:12:41,291
I-- Here I treat like the collectors,
exporters, and the backends, as

195
00:12:41,301 --> 00:12:43,661
non-critical path dependencies.

196
00:12:44,481 --> 00:12:45,831
So look at the two states.

197
00:12:46,411 --> 00:12:50,441
On the left side, the telemetry is
healthy, like the request returns

198
00:12:50,451 --> 00:12:53,081
two hundred, and spans are captured.

199
00:12:53,671 --> 00:12:54,901
Full trace is available.

200
00:12:55,321 --> 00:13:00,331
On the right, the collector is down, and
the request still returns two hundred.

201
00:13:00,791 --> 00:13:03,861
spans are dropped, the request
is completely unaffected.

202
00:13:04,251 --> 00:13:08,391
So you lose observability data,
but you do not lose user traffic.

203
00:13:08,891 --> 00:13:11,621
And this has to be enforced
in the architecture.

204
00:13:11,991 --> 00:13:16,731
it should not be left to a, like
a well-behaved SDK or a hopeful

205
00:13:16,891 --> 00:13:21,781
timeout config because, here the
failure mode where your monitoring

206
00:13:21,781 --> 00:13:25,601
takes down your product is one of the
most embarrassing outages there is.

207
00:13:26,101 --> 00:13:28,701
So we have all seen it or
heard the story, right?

208
00:13:28,701 --> 00:13:28,831
Right.

209
00:13:29,681 --> 00:13:33,421
so here the collector gets
overwhelmed, export calls start

210
00:13:33,441 --> 00:13:37,441
blo-blocking, and then the threads
pile up waiting for the telemetry.

211
00:13:37,501 --> 00:13:42,181
And suddenly, the thing that you added
to understand outages is causing one.

212
00:13:43,111 --> 00:13:45,441
So it can be, like, entirely avoidable.

213
00:13:45,931 --> 00:13:50,461
And the way you avoid it is to make
telemetry structural-- structurally

214
00:13:50,471 --> 00:13:52,251
incapable of blocking a request.

215
00:13:53,611 --> 00:13:58,411
then the next slide moving on to,
like, in this slide, I'm talking

216
00:13:58,411 --> 00:14:03,251
about, like, the three design, three,
compliance frameworks, which follow--

217
00:14:03,281 --> 00:14:05,031
which can follow one single design.

218
00:14:05,081 --> 00:14:09,841
because, the, because, the identity,
is consistent and the metadata is

219
00:14:09,841 --> 00:14:14,541
controlled, one design can map cleanly
onto three compliance frameworks,

220
00:14:14,561 --> 00:14:20,821
like the SOC 2, which, deterministic
traces give auditors a reproducible,

221
00:14:21,101 --> 00:14:22,841
replayable record of what happened.

222
00:14:23,301 --> 00:14:27,951
And then HIPAA, the allow list
excludes payloads and user data,

223
00:14:28,281 --> 00:14:32,091
so protected health information
never reaches a trace store.

224
00:14:32,701 --> 00:14:34,121
Then PCI DSS.

225
00:14:34,751 --> 00:14:38,831
Deny by default means, like, card
numbers and credentials can't

226
00:14:38,841 --> 00:14:40,431
ride along in span attributes.

227
00:14:41,301 --> 00:14:45,991
And here's why this matters, like, and why
you have to, like, spend your time here.

228
00:14:46,951 --> 00:14:51,831
Normally, each of these frameworks
pulls a separate audit, a separate

229
00:14:51,871 --> 00:14:53,271
control, a separate scramble.

230
00:14:54,111 --> 00:14:58,291
When the control is structural
and when the architecture itself

231
00:14:58,441 --> 00:15:02,461
makes the bad outcome impossible,
you answer the auditor's question

232
00:15:02,471 --> 00:15:04,691
once, and it holds across all three.

233
00:15:05,091 --> 00:15:08,481
So you're not building here, like,
three separate compliance stories.

234
00:15:08,721 --> 00:15:10,101
It's the same architecture.

235
00:15:10,241 --> 00:15:11,621
It answers all three.

236
00:15:12,841 --> 00:15:18,391
So for a, for a DevSecOps team, that's
the difference between, security being a

237
00:15:18,391 --> 00:15:24,551
tax on every release and security being
something the platform just does Then, the

238
00:15:24,551 --> 00:15:27,731
seven capabilities, the system guarantees.

239
00:15:27,911 --> 00:15:30,091
So here's where, I make
the claims testable.

240
00:15:31,201 --> 00:15:34,631
seven capabilities, the system
guarantees, like each one

241
00:15:34,701 --> 00:15:36,781
independently, can be verifiable.

242
00:15:37,301 --> 00:15:40,541
the first four are, like,
h-about, reliable tracing.

243
00:15:41,221 --> 00:15:45,711
one is every request, it gets a
globally unique trace ID at ingress,

244
00:15:46,211 --> 00:15:48,221
and, existing IDs are preserved.

245
00:15:48,661 --> 00:15:55,541
Two, each request creates its own span
with correct parent-child lineage, and

246
00:15:55,541 --> 00:15:57,851
retries, keep the original trace ID.

247
00:15:59,301 --> 00:16:05,151
Third, full trace-level data capture
that, that includes, like IDs, names,

248
00:16:05,721 --> 00:16:08,231
timings, status, and HTTP code.

249
00:16:08,551 --> 00:16:13,401
And the fourth one, you can query
any trace, by its ID, and see

250
00:16:13,401 --> 00:16:15,011
the complete end-to-end path.

251
00:16:16,821 --> 00:16:21,671
Then, the f-- last three, like, they're
about, being operable and secure.

252
00:16:22,371 --> 00:16:25,341
the fifth one here is config-only export.

253
00:16:25,861 --> 00:16:29,191
Destination set via a Kubernetes config.

254
00:16:29,811 --> 00:16:34,811
and there would be no code changes, with
multiple backend and tenant routing.

255
00:16:35,241 --> 00:16:37,661
And the sixth one, rich, visualization.

256
00:16:37,701 --> 00:16:41,481
Like, you can see the end-to-end
trace views, dependency graphs,

257
00:16:41,561 --> 00:16:44,271
and per-span latency breakdowns.

258
00:16:44,681 --> 00:16:47,341
The seventh one is
non-disruptive and secure.

259
00:16:47,941 --> 00:16:52,611
Tracing never blocks requests when the
backend is down, and trace data excludes

260
00:16:52,611 --> 00:16:53,881
payloads, credentials, and PII by design.

261
00:16:54,641 --> 00:16:58,831
And because each of these, is
independently testable, they

262
00:16:58,831 --> 00:17:01,011
are automatable in CI/CD.

263
00:17:02,101 --> 00:17:04,261
it's not about like, are we tracing ready?

264
00:17:04,321 --> 00:17:08,741
Like, it's, it stops, being a
judgment call, and it becomes

265
00:17:08,741 --> 00:17:10,301
a checklist i-you, you run.

266
00:17:10,851 --> 00:17:14,361
let's, let me make this like
a concrete with the scenario.

267
00:17:14,461 --> 00:17:18,181
so it's the exact pattern
this architecture eliminates.

268
00:17:18,261 --> 00:17:23,051
So, checkout, like, let's say like the
checkout starts failing intermittently.

269
00:17:23,711 --> 00:17:27,341
Roughly one in twenty request
returns a five hundred.

270
00:17:27,981 --> 00:17:32,621
No pattern in the dashboards, and
it only reproduces under load.

271
00:17:32,621 --> 00:17:34,741
So here now the hunt begins.

272
00:17:34,821 --> 00:17:39,821
Like, Engineer A opens the cart
service logs, sees the database

273
00:17:39,831 --> 00:17:41,951
timeouts and blames the database.

274
00:17:42,011 --> 00:17:45,251
And then Engineer B looks at
the payment service metrics.

275
00:17:45,591 --> 00:17:50,301
They see elevated, latency
and blames a downstream API.

276
00:17:51,191 --> 00:17:51,971
So here's the thing.

277
00:17:52,451 --> 00:17:57,871
They are both looking at real signals,
but from this s- same failure, and they

278
00:17:57,951 --> 00:18:00,801
reach, they reach opposite conclusions.

279
00:18:00,901 --> 00:18:05,791
So, that takes, like, hours of two
people chasing two wrong answers.

280
00:18:06,281 --> 00:18:12,681
Now, here again, the deterministic version
is pull one failing trace by its ID.

281
00:18:13,241 --> 00:18:15,451
The waterfall shows the truth immediately.

282
00:18:15,451 --> 00:18:19,601
Like, authentication service is
exhausting its connection pool under load.

283
00:18:20,091 --> 00:18:23,551
like it, it, it is delaying
every downstream hop.

284
00:18:23,901 --> 00:18:27,891
Then the database timeout and the
payment latency were both symptoms.

285
00:18:28,381 --> 00:18:30,291
They are not the actual root cause.

286
00:18:30,541 --> 00:18:37,071
So one trace, one answer, and then d- the
diagnostic shifted from hours to minutes

287
00:18:38,071 --> 00:18:42,221
So, a, a few honest lessons, I learned
from running this in production.

288
00:18:42,751 --> 00:18:48,951
First, the constraints shape the
design, and the mandatory propagation

289
00:18:48,981 --> 00:18:52,771
and ingress level identity weren't
the elegant academic choice.

290
00:18:53,011 --> 00:18:57,621
They were the choice that survived
real services owned by the teams.

291
00:18:58,131 --> 00:19:02,761
and the second is, you have to design
failure handling in from day one.

292
00:19:03,281 --> 00:19:06,831
So non-disruptive telemetry
failure cannot be retrofitted.

293
00:19:07,711 --> 00:19:12,431
If export sits on the request path
even once, you will find out during

294
00:19:12,441 --> 00:19:15,021
your worst outage and not before.

295
00:19:15,731 --> 00:19:19,301
And the third is, like, security
is cheaper when it's structural.

296
00:19:20,121 --> 00:19:23,441
Like, bolting metadata
controls on after launch means

297
00:19:23,561 --> 00:19:26,021
auditing every existing field.

298
00:19:26,611 --> 00:19:29,011
So you deny by default from the start.

299
00:19:29,361 --> 00:19:30,861
It's, it's the one-time game.

300
00:19:31,251 --> 00:19:33,691
Not-- It's not a, a perpetual cleanup

301
00:19:34,691 --> 00:19:37,371
So let me boil it down to four numbers.

302
00:19:38,181 --> 00:19:42,951
so far, like we have covered the seven
capabilities which are all automatable

303
00:19:42,951 --> 00:19:48,911
in CI/CD, and nine allowlist metadata
fields for compliance safe richness,

304
00:19:49,291 --> 00:19:53,841
and then three compliance frameworks,
covered by one design, and zero

305
00:19:53,841 --> 00:19:55,711
request impact on telemetry failure.

306
00:19:56,191 --> 00:20:01,351
So that last hero, like it's
non-negotiable, is a whole resilience

307
00:20:01,371 --> 00:20:03,271
contract in, in a single digit

308
00:20:04,271 --> 00:20:09,091
If you, if you take one thing home, it's
that the combination is what matters.

309
00:20:09,481 --> 00:20:14,381
So ingress-level identity plus
mandatory propagation, plus, allowlisted

310
00:20:14,421 --> 00:20:20,031
metadata, plus config-driven controls,
plus non-disruptive failure handling.

311
00:20:20,491 --> 00:20:24,621
Any one of these alone is a,
a nice-to-have, but you-- when

312
00:20:24,621 --> 00:20:28,631
you put, all of them together,
that's what makes the diagnostics

313
00:20:28,661 --> 00:20:31,201
deterministic, secure, and resilient.

314
00:20:32,501 --> 00:20:33,761
And here are my key takeaways.

315
00:20:34,001 --> 00:20:39,331
Like, one is, like, determinism is an
architectural, architecture problem.

316
00:20:39,801 --> 00:20:41,381
it's not a tooling problem.

317
00:20:41,741 --> 00:20:45,821
mandatory propagation and ingress
identity are what separate, sep…

318
00:20:46,401 --> 00:20:50,871
These are the ones that separate reliable
tracing from aspirational instrumentation.

319
00:20:51,241 --> 00:20:54,271
And the second one is security
is structural, it's not additive.

320
00:20:54,781 --> 00:20:57,971
and the allowlist is part of the
design, and it's not a filter

321
00:20:58,281 --> 00:20:59,811
you bolt on after the leak.

322
00:21:00,101 --> 00:21:03,831
And the three-- third one is resilience
is a contract, it's not a goal.

323
00:21:04,451 --> 00:21:09,391
Zero request impact, it has to be
enforced and, verified, and not assumed.

324
00:21:11,051 --> 00:21:11,551
that's it.

325
00:21:11,851 --> 00:21:13,031
thank you, thank you.

326
00:21:13,121 --> 00:21:14,941
and, I'm happy to take any questions.

327
00:21:15,261 --> 00:21:19,061
if you want to go deeper on the
seven capabilities or allowlists,

328
00:21:19,111 --> 00:21:22,951
fields, you can, find me on
LinkedIn, and you can reach out to

